is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The inter-VLAN network path between 172.16.20.0/24 (your Laptop0) and 172.16.10.0/24 (PC4 and peers) is healthy; there is no ban or block in the routing/switching layer. Evidence:
- Laptop0 (172.16.20.10) can ping PC4 (172.16.10.4) with 0% loss and low RTT, and vice versa. TCP also works across VLANs (iperf from Laptop0 to PC4 sustained ~44 Mbit/s with 0 retransmits).
- Router0 is routing both VLANs on subinterfaces eth0.20 (172.16.20.1) and eth0.10 (172.16.10.1) and shows no filtering (iptables: ACCEPT on all chains). Switch1 bridges VLAN10 and VLAN20 correctly with no ebtables or iptables filters.
- “Ignoring” at the application layer is expected on PC4 because it isn’t running any server services (netstat on PC4 shows no listeners other than the internal resolver on 127.0.0.11). A curl test to http://172.16.10.4 from Laptop0 completes immediately (no service to answer), which matches the lack of a web daemon.

Mechanism: Since L3/L2 are open, ICMP/TCP packets reach the destination, but application ports you expect (e.g., web/print/ssh) aren’t listening or are blocked by the endpoint’s host firewall, leading to the perception of being “banned.”

Localization: Network path (Laptop0 → Switch1 → mswitch1 → Router0 → VLAN10) is fine; the issue is at the destination hosts’ service layer (e.g., PC4).

Root cause: Required services on 172.16.10.x hosts (e.g., PC4) are not running/listening, not a router ACL.

Corrective fix: Start/enable the needed services on PC4/other 172.16.10.x devices and/or adjust their host firewalls to allow access from 172.16.20.0/24; no changes needed on routers/switches.
=== END ===